Hi Shanruo,

Ah, I understand now. My first reaction would be that there is no real
need to remove the imputed values of C. Only in fairly rare
circumstances is it better to eliminate those cases that are missing
on C than to use the imputed values. In fact, dropping them could
produce a large amount of bias, even at only 10% of your data. Having
said that, you can use the combination rules if you did use the
original C instead of the imputed C.

You are correct on point (2).

For point (3), as long as you have covariates, you should definitely
use the imputations when A and B are missing. The scenario under which
you would not use the imputations would be when you think that those
missing observations are very different than the observed values, in
ways that cannot be predicted with the covariates. But if this is true
(we call this non-ignorable missing data), then you will have bias and
inefficiency whether or not you use the imputed values. For more on
this point, you may want to look at the King, et al (2001) paper.

Hope that helps.

Cheers,
matt.

